      "durationSeconds": 54.48,
      "transcript": "Prince Michael Street. Not just shopfronts, but the ridge pulling the city west. Read Prince Michael Street less as a shopping street and more as a line. Stand in the middle and look ahead. The street seems to pull you toward Kalamegdan. Under the easy surface is an older logic, the stable upper shelf of the center. The facades, windows, musicians, and cafe noise make Belgrade feel almost calm here. That calm matters. It shows how a border city learned to live not only through war and height, but through civic everyday life. The river is still hidden. The street is the breath before the edge. How the street draws the eye toward Kalamegdan. The shift from center to direction. Fatads that make a frontier city feel composed. Look along the street axis more than into the windows. If the crowd is thick, pause on a wider stretch and look west. Continue west, then turn toward Kozantsev-Venak and the viewpoint edge above the Sava.",
